Yes, it works. It is posted as "[PATCH v3] mail: Add just am to apply
patches in any workspace" [1]. The mailinfo parsing is gone. am.sh now
adds a worktree under $TMPDIR at the workspace's parent of @, runs git
am --empty=drop there, and runs jj new on the result in the workspace.
A trap on EXIT removes the worktree. It is also more correct than v2:
if a patch fails, nothing is applied, where v2 kept the patches before
it.

One thing your outline did not cover. jj can only see the commits git
am makes through a ref, so the worktree is on a temporary branch,
which jj imports. The trap deletes that branch too. A plain import
after that makes jj abandon the new commits, and their base, as
unreachable. My first scratch run lost both that way. So the trap
imports with git.abandon-unreachable-commits=false, and the patches
stay visible as ancestors of the new @.

